Draft profit of Hub Chowki Feeds Limited for the year ended 31 March 2027 is $274,800. At 1 April 2026 retained earnings were $318,000 and the general reserve $60,000. It is discovered that:
• dividends paid of $64,000 were included in administrative expenses;
• a gain of $45,000 on revaluing land was included in other income;
• the directors' decision to transfer $30,000 to the general reserve has not been recorded.

Kasur Leather Limited had 2,400,000 ordinary shares on 1 April 2026. During the year ended 31 March 2027:
• May 2026: final dividend for the previous year, 4.5 cents per share;
• August 2026: bonus issue of 1 share for every 6 held;
• January 2027: interim dividend of 3 cents per share.

Kaghan Juices began trading on 1 January 2025. It produces 20,000 cartons a year. Data:
• selling price $25 per carton; variable production cost $14 per carton;
• fixed production overheads $80,000 a year, absorbed on 20,000 cartons;
• sales: 2025 17,000 cartons; 2026 21,000 cartons.
There are no other costs.

Data for Mardan Tools for April:
• production 8,000 units; sales 7,000 units at $40; no opening inventory;
• variable production cost $18 per unit; variable selling cost $2 per unit sold;
• fixed production overheads $48,000 (absorbed on actual production);
• fixed administration and selling costs $30,000.

Gulmarg Shoes budgeted fixed production overheads of $120,000 for the year and budgeted production of 24,000 pairs. Overheads are absorbed per pair. Actual results for the year:
• production 22,000 pairs; sales 20,000 pairs at $30; no opening inventory;
• variable production cost $14 per pair; variable selling cost $1 per pair sold;
• actual fixed production overheads $118,000; fixed administration costs $40,000.

Margalla Marble transfers finished goods from its factory at production cost plus 30%. For the year ended 31 December 2026:
• production cost of goods completed $520,000;
• revenue $890,000;
• finished goods inventory at transfer price: 1 January 2026 $71,500; 31 December 2026 $58,500;
• administration and selling expenses $164,000.

Indus Bottlers transfers goods from its factory to its sales division at production cost plus 20%. The factory manager argues that the transfer should be at market value, which is 10% below the current transfer price. Year ended 30 September 2026:
• production cost of goods completed $600,000;
• revenue $1,050,000; finished goods: no opening inventory; closing inventory 5,000 units with production cost $25 each;
• all units are transferred at the same price per unit.

Crescent Cycles has decided whether to close its factory and buy all its bicycles from an outside supplier at the market price, which it currently uses as its transfer price. Year ended 31 December 2026:
• 8,000 bicycles completed, transferred at market price of $150 each;
• production cost of goods completed $1,020,000, made up of: direct materials $480,000, direct labour $296,000, variable factory overheads $84,000, fixed factory overheads $160,000;
• if the factory closed, $70,000 of the fixed factory overheads would be avoided; the rest is depreciation and head-office charges that would continue.

Quetta Pumps provides the following for the year ended 31 December 2026:
• factory machinery cost $180,000, accumulated depreciation at 1 January 2026 $64,800; depreciation is 20% reducing balance;
• wages paid in the year $214,000, of which 85% was for direct labour and 15% for indirect labour;
• direct wages accrued: 1 January 2026 $4,500; 31 December 2026 $6,000 (no indirect wages were accrued);
• other factory overheads $41,700.

Saeed Glassworks' year ends on 31 December 2026. Factory expense details:
• factory power: paid $31,200; owing at 1 January 2026 $2,400; owing at 31 December 2026 $3,100;
• factory insurance: paid $9,600; prepaid at 1 January 2026 $1,800; prepaid at 31 December 2026 $2,100;
• factory rent: $24,000 a year; only $20,000 was paid during the year;
• indirect wages paid $38,000.

At 31 May 2026 the inventories of Delta Electricals Ltd at cost were: raw materials $32,000; work in progress $18,750; finished goods $61,200. Further information:
• raw materials include an obsolete batch of cable costing $3,500 that can be sold only as scrap for $900;
• finished goods include damaged units costing $4,000 that can be sold for $3,600 after repair costs of $400.

Cedar Furniture, Beirut, lost its detailed cost records in a flood. The following is known for the year:
• revenue was $720,000 and the gross margin was 30%;
• finished goods inventory was $41,000 at the start and $37,500 at the end of the year;
• work in progress was $12,400 at the start and $15,900 at the end of the year;
• factory overheads were 25% of prime cost;
• direct labour was 40% of prime cost, and there were no direct expenses.

Lotus Electronics Ltd made 12,000 units of a product in the year ended 31 December 2026. Normal capacity is 16,000 units a year. Production costs were:
• direct materials $216,000, including $6,000 of materials destroyed in a fire (abnormal wastage);
• direct labour $144,000, including $4,800 paid for idle time during a strike (abnormal);
• fixed production overheads $96,000.
At the year end 2,500 units were unsold. They sell for $42 each and selling costs are $3 per unit. The company follows IAS 2 Inventories.

At 30 September 2026, the year end of Hina's clothing boutique in Islamabad:
1 advertising of $1,750 had been paid for a magazine campaign running from 1 September to 30 November 2026;
2 commission of $940 was due to Hina from a fabric supplier for sales she introduced in September but had not yet been received;
3 security services of $480 for September had not been paid.

Usman supplies spare parts on 60 days' credit to garages in Lahore. Annual credit sales are $900,000 and on average 2.5% of credit sales become irrecoverable. Trade receivables average $148,000 and are financed by a bank overdraft costing 15% a year.
Usman proposes to offer a cash discount of 2% to customers who pay within 10 days. He expects:
• customers making 60% of credit sales to take the discount;
• average trade receivables to fall to $70,000;
• irrecoverable debts to fall to 1% of credit sales.

Yusuf's financial year ended on 31 December 2026, but his inventory was not counted until 7 January 2027, when it was valued at cost at $36,850. Between 1 and 7 January 2027:
• sales were $6,600; Yusuf sells all goods at cost plus 20%;
• purchases received were $3,140;
• goods with a selling price of $480 were returned by customers and put back into inventory;
• goods costing $260 were returned to suppliers.
Goods costing $900 included in the count were found to have a net realisable value of $520.

A medical testing laboratory in Karachi has the following monthly costs:
• fixed costs (rent, salaries, insurance) $36,000;
• machine maintenance: a contract of $4,000 per month plus $1.50 per test;
• reagents and test kits: $6.00 per test.

Al-Burhan Electricals, Faisalabad, buys components costing $1,200,000 a year. A supplier offers to deliver daily on a just-in-time basis but will charge 1.5% more for the components. If JIT is adopted:
• average inventory will fall from $200,000 to $50,000; the cash released will repay part of a loan that costs 9% interest a year;
• a rented store costing $14,000 a year will no longer be needed;
• obsolete inventory written off will fall from $11,000 to $2,000 a year;
• extra administration of deliveries will cost $8,000 a year.

A private hospital in Karachi is considering just-in-time ordering for its pharmacy. Currently:
• average inventory of medicines is $600,000; holding costs are 10% of average inventory a year;
• medicines that pass their expiry date cost $30,000 a year.
Under JIT, with two daily deliveries from a wholesaler:
• average inventory would be $150,000;
• expired medicines would cost $8,000 a year;
• the wholesaler estimates that a medicine would be out of stock on 6 occasions a year, and each time the hospital would have to buy from a retail pharmacy at an extra cost of $3,500.

Margalla Fittings, Islamabad, makes one product. Data for a typical week:
• 50 workers each work 40 hours at $10 per hour and produce 6 units per hour;
• selling price $9 per unit; direct materials $3 per unit;
• fixed production overheads $36,000 per week.
The directors propose a piece rate of $1.80 per unit. Output is expected to rise by 15% and extra quality inspection will cost $2,000 per week. All units can be sold.
